  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBrief upper ridging between systems today, then another trough\nmoving sharply south-eastward through the Plains Tuesday and\nWednesday. High pressure currently over the region will move\nsoutheast and southerly winds will pick up. The main upper low\nwill be well to our southeast by Wednesday night into Thursday,\nbut another, more northerly branch shortwave digs down on\nFriday. Northwesterly flow aloft for Saturday, then more west \nto northwesterly Sunday into Monday.\n\n...Light snow in south Tuesday...\n\nMain surface reflection from Tuesday's upper low is an inverted\ntrough setting up along the SD/Neb border with southerly winds\nbringing light precip into the eastern Dakotas. A bit better\nagreement between the ensemble members on light precipitation\nmoving through. Best chances this time look to be south of the\nHighway 200 corridor for precipitation. The NBM and REFS \nensembles have probabilities of at least a tenth of an inch of\nsnow ranging from 40 to 60 percent. Probabilities of over an\ninch are minimal, but could see a light dusting of snow like\nyesterday and given that portion of the CWA didn't get any with\nthe last round, may be worth mentioning. \n\n...Temperatures warming towards the end of the week...\n\nNot expecting readings to drop quite as low as last night due to\nincreasing winds and cloud cover, but should still be plenty\ncold in the single digits to teens overnight. Southerly winds\nwill bring highs back up to near seasonal averages tomorrow in\nthe low 30s, then a slow warming trend into Thursday and Friday.\nBack down again a bit after Friday's trough passes, but still on\nthe warmer side of what is normal for late November.",
